  "preamble": "The shift from the voiced velar stop [ɡ] to the voiced velar fricative [ɣ] (gamma) is a classic form of lenition known as spirantization. It most frequently occurs in intervocalic or post-vocalic positions where the closure required for a stop is incomplete, allowing continuous voiced airflow.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Spirantization, Lenition",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Spanish",
      "languageFamily": "Romance",
      "isAllophone": true,
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "agua /ˈaɡwa/ 'water'",
          "to": "[ˈa.ɣ̞wa]",
          "note": "Intervocalic /ɡ/ is realized as an approximant or fricative."
        },
        {
          "from": "lago /ˈlaɡo/ 'lake'",
          "to": "[ˈla.ɣ̞o]"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Khalkha Mongolian",
      "languageFamily": "Mongolic",
      "isAllophone": true,
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "ger [ɡɛr] (house)",
          "to": "[ɣɛr]",
          "note": "In Khalkha Mongolian, /g/ is realized as [ɣ] in intervocalic position and between sonorants. The stop allophone [ɡ] appears word-initially and after nasals."
        },
        {
          "from": "egch [eɡtʃ] (older sister)",
          "to": "[eɣtʃ]",
          "note": "Intervocalic /g/ spirantizes to [ɣ] in medial consonant clusters when preceded by a vowel. Attested across Khalkha and Inner Mongolian varieties."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Biblical Hebrew",
      "languageFamily": "Semitic",
      "isAllophone": true,
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "גָּדוֹל gādōl [ɡ] (great, word-initial)",
          "to": "[ɣādōl] (post-vocalic in connected speech)",
          "note": "Beged-Kefet spirantization: /g/ (gimel) is realized as [ɡ] word-initially or after a consonant, and as [ɣ] after a vowel in connected speech. The distinction is marked with a dagesh (dot) in the Biblical text: גּ [ɡ] vs. ג [ɣ]. Attested in Tiberian Hebrew vocalization (c. 7th–10th century CE) and retained in Yemenite Hebrew pronunciation."
        }
      ]
    },
